window.docContents[92].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___44-Other-Applications/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___44-Other-Applications--___4___44-Other-Applications":{"contents":"This page is an applications overview and contains no numbered mathematical\ndisplays. It records where elementary combinations of exponentials and\nlogarithms enter applied models, and points to the relevant references and\nneighboring DLMF sections.\n\nThe current DLMF version notes that the paragraph on the Einstein functions\nand Planck's radiation function was added effective with version 1.0.3. The\naddition also records the associated references and adds a reference to\nCorless et al. in the final paragraph.\n\nThe Einstein functions and Planck's radiation function are elementary\ncombinations of exponentials, or of exponentials and logarithms. DLMF points\nto Abramowitz and Stegun (1964, pp. 999–1000) and Ashcroft and Mermin (1976,\nChapter 23).\n\nApplications of generalized exponentials and generalized logarithms to\ncomputer arithmetic are referred to DLMF §3.1(iv). Applications of the\nLambert W-function to generalized Gaussian noise are referenced to\nChapeau-Blondeau and Monir (2002).\n\nFor other applications of the Lambert W-function, DLMF refers to Corless et\nal. (1996). The page links forward to §4.45, Methods of Computation, and back\nto §4.43, Cubic Equations.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.44 Other Applications","header":"§4.44 Other Applications","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___44-Other-Applications/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___44-Other-Applications--___4___44-Other-Applications"}});